  "name": "ANTISTREPTOLYSIN O (ASO) LATEX AGGLUTINATION",
  "code": "1035",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"175\" data-end=\"356\">The <strong data-start=\"179\" data-end=\"233\">ASO (Anti-Streptolysin O) Latex Agglutination Test</strong> detects <strong data-start=\"242\" data-end=\"256\">antibodies</strong> against <strong data-start=\"265\" data-end=\"283\">streptolysin O</strong>, a toxin produced by <strong data-start=\"305\" data-end=\"355\">Group A Streptococcus (Streptococcus pyogenes)</strong>.</p>\n<p data-start=\"358\" data-end=\"392\">This test is clinically used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"393\" data-end=\"709\">\n<li data-start=\"393\" data-end=\"504\">\n<p data-start=\"395\" data-end=\"504\">Detect a <strong data-start=\"404\" data-end=\"438\">recent streptococcal infection</strong>, especially when the infection is <strong data-start=\"473\" data-end=\"502\">not currently symptomatic</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"505\" data-end=\"709\">\n<p data-start=\"507\" data-end=\"575\">Support diagnosis of <strong data-start=\"528\" data-end=\"564\">post-streptococcal complications</strong> such as:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"578\" data-end=\"709\">\n<li data-start=\"578\" data-end=\"601\">\n<p data-start=\"580\" data-end=\"601\"><strong data-start=\"580\" data-end=\"599\">Rheumatic fever</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"604\" data-end=\"656\">\n<p data-start=\"606\" data-end=\"656\"><strong data-start=\"606\" data-end=\"654\">Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN)</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"659\" data-end=\"709\">\n<p data-start=\"661\" data-end=\"709\"><strong data-start=\"661\" data-end=\"678\">Scarlet fever</strong> or <strong data-start=\"682\" data-end=\"709\">streptococcal arthritis</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"711\" data-end=\"847\">It is especially helpful when throat cultures are negative but symptoms of autoimmune complications appear weeks after initial infection.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Recent streptococcal pharyngitis or skin infection</strong></td>\n<td>Antibody formation typically rises 1&ndash;3 weeks post-infection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rheumatic fever</strong></td>\n<td>High ASO supports recent streptococcal trigger for autoimmune cardiac/joint damage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis</strong></td>\n<td>Supports immune-mediated kidney damage following streptococcal infection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Streptococcal arthritis or scarlet fever</strong></td>\n<td>Indicates prior exposure in systemic immune response conditions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote data-start=\"1742\" data-end=\"1922\">\n<p data-start=\"1744\" data-end=\"1922\"><strong data-start=\"1744\" data-end=\"1753\">Note:</strong> A single ASO test has limited diagnostic value; <strong data-start=\"1802\" data-end=\"1819\">serial titres</strong> or paired samples may be used to confirm <strong data-start=\"1861\" data-end=\"1878\">rising titres</strong>, which are more diagnostically significant.</p>\n</blockquote>",
